    slug: "sewer-camera-inspection",
    title: "Free Sewer Video Camera Inspection",
    shortDesc: "We show you your sewer line on the spot, in minutes. Our free sewer video camera inspection is included with every service call, a $400 value at no charge to you.",
    longDesc: "Most drain and sewer problems are invisible until they cause real damage. A slow drain might be a minor grease buildup, or it might be a collapsed section of pipe 10 feet underground. The only way to know is to look.\n\nWe include a free sewer video camera inspection with every service call. Our technicians push a high-resolution camera through your drain line and show you exactly what is happening in real time on a monitor at the job site. You see what we see. There is no guessing, no upselling based on symptoms alone, and no recommending repairs you do not actually need.\n\nThe camera records footage of the full length of the accessible pipe, documenting the condition of joints, the presence of root intrusion, any cracks or offsets, and the location of any blockages. After the inspection, we walk you through the footage and explain what we found in plain language. If there is a problem, we tell you what it is, where it is, and what your options are. If the pipe is in good shape, we tell you that too.\n\nFor homeowners buying or selling a property in London Ontario, a pre-purchase sewer inspection is one of the most valuable things you can do. Many older homes in London have clay tile sewer lines that are decades old. A camera inspection before you close can reveal problems that a standard home inspection will miss entirely.\n\nThe inspection is free. The information it gives you is not.",
    fullDesc: null,
    benefits: "[{\"desc\": \"A sewer camera inspection is a $400 value included at no charge with every Discount Drain service call. You see exactly what is in your pipe before any work begins.\", \"title\": \"Free With Every Service Call\"}, {\"desc\": \"The camera footage is displayed on a monitor at the job site. You watch the inspection as it happens and see the same footage our technician sees.\", \"title\": \"Real-Time On-Site Viewing\"}, {\"desc\": \"The inspection is recorded so you have documentation of your pipe condition. Useful for insurance claims, property sales, and tracking changes over time.\", \"title\": \"Recorded Documentation\"}, {\"desc\": \"Before buying a home in London Ontario, a camera inspection of the sewer line can reveal clay tile failures, root intrusion, and other problems that standard home inspections miss.\", \"title\": \"Pre-Purchase Inspections\"}, {\"desc\": \"The camera feeds to a monitor on site. You see exactly what is in your pipe in real time, so you understand the problem before we recommend any solution.\", \"title\": \"You Watch the Footage Live\"}, {\"desc\": \"The camera travels the full length of your private sewer line to the connection point. If the issue is in the city portion, we identify that clearly and explain your options.\", \"title\": \"Identifies City vs. Private Responsibility\"}]",
    faqs: "[{\"q\":\"How much does a sewer camera inspection cost in London Ontario?\",\"a\":\"Many companies in London charge $200 to $400 for a standalone sewer camera inspection. At Discount Drain, we include a free video camera inspection with every service call, so you pay nothing extra to see exactly what is happening in your pipe.\"},{\"q\":\"How long does a sewer camera inspection take?\",\"a\":\"Most residential inspections take 30 to 60 minutes from start to finish. The camera is fed through the cleanout access point and travels the full length of the accessible pipe. You watch the footage live on a monitor as we go.\"},{\"q\":\"What can a sewer camera inspection find?\",\"a\":\"The camera can identify root intrusion, cracks and fractures in the pipe wall, collapsed or partially collapsed sections, offset joints where pipe sections have shifted, bellied pipe (sections that have sagged and hold standing water), grease and scale buildup, and foreign objects lodged in the line.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need a sewer camera inspection before buying a house in Ontario?\",\"a\":\"A standard home inspection does not include the sewer line. In older London neighbourhoods where clay tile pipes are common, a camera inspection before closing is one of the most useful things you can do. If the camera finds root intrusion or a cracked pipe, you have room to negotiate or ask the seller to address it before you sign.\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the cleanout access point for a sewer camera inspection?\",\"a\":\"The cleanout is usually in the basement, the utility room, or outside near the foundation. If your home does not have an accessible cleanout, we can access the line through a roof vent or by removing a toilet. We will find the right access point before starting.\"},{\"q\":\"Will the camera inspection tell me if the problem is on city property?\",\"a\":\"Yes. The camera travels the full length of the private sewer line from your house to the connection point. If the main issue is in the city's portion of the pipe (the Private Drain Connection), we will identify that clearly. The City of London has a flat-rate PDC renewal program at $7,250 as of 2026, payable over 10 years through your property taxes.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a sewer camera inspection be done on any type of pipe?\",\"a\":\"Yes. The camera works in clay tile, cast iron, PVC, ABS, and concrete pipe. The diameter of the pipe determines which camera head we use. Most residential sewer lines in London range from 4 to 6 inches in diameter.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens after the inspection if a problem is found?\",\"a\":\"We explain what we found, show you the footage, and walk through your options. Depending on the issue, that might be a cleaning, a pipe lining, a spot repair, or in more serious cases, excavation and replacement. You get the information first and make the decision from there.\"},{\"q\":\"Can tree roots really damage a sewer line?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and it is the most common finding in older London homes. Roots enter through small cracks or joint gaps in clay tile pipes and grow over time. They can partially or fully block the line. If roots are found, we will tell you whether cleaning is sufficient or whether lining the pipe makes more sense to prevent the roots from returning.\"},{\"q\":\"How often should I have a sewer camera inspection done?\",\"a\":\"If you have an older clay tile sewer line and mature trees near the line, every two to three years is a reasonable schedule. If you have already had root intrusion, annual inspection is worth considering. For newer PVC lines with no history of problems, inspection at the time of purchase and then as needed is typically sufficient.\"},{\"q\":\"Is a sewer camera inspection covered by home insurance in Ontario?\",\"a\":\"The inspection itself is not typically covered by insurance. However, if the inspection reveals damage that qualifies under your policy's sewer backup or water damage coverage, the repair work may be covered. Check your policy or call your broker before assuming coverage either way.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between a sewer camera inspection and a drain scope?\",\"a\":\"They are the same thing described differently. A sewer camera inspection, drain scope, sewer scope, and CCTV inspection all refer to the process of running a camera through the pipe to view its condition. The terms are used interchangeably in the industry.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I get a recording of the inspection footage?\",\"a\":\"Yes. We can provide footage from the inspection so you have a record of the pipe condition. This is particularly useful for home buyers who want documentation to share with a real estate agent or insurance broker.\"}]",
